var a=e=>`<mi>${e}</mi>`,i=e=>`<mo>${e}</mo>`,s=(e,t)=>`<msub>${e}<mtext>${t}</mtext></msub>`,r=(e,t)=>`<mfrac><mrow>${e}</mrow><mrow>${t}</mrow></mfrac>`,n={label:"Stent diameter",param:"tips.d",min:6,max:12,step:1,unit:"mm"},o={id:"shunts",level:"advanced",title:"TIPS and shunts",minutes:12,objectives:["Recognize a large spontaneous portosystemic shunt by the hepatic encephalopathy it causes","Name the indications for TIPS and the pre-TIPS evaluation","Explain the effects of TIPS on portal pressure, hepatic perfusion and the heart, why the stent is kept narrow, and how TIPS dysfunction presents","Explain how the selective Warren shunt decompresses the varices while preserving portal flow to the liver"],checks:[["An 8 mm TIPS is dilated to 10 mm. The PPG falls slightly further and hepatic portal perfusion falls. What did the extra 2 mm gain, and at what cost?","A small further fall in the gradient, at the cost of less hepatic portal perfusion, so hepatic encephalopathy and liver failure become more likely. That is why stents are usually 8 to 10 mm and not wider."],["A 72-year-old woman has refractory ascites and no heart failure. Is her age a reason to rule out a TIPS?","No. At age \u226570 a TIPS is judged case by case, weighing benefit and risk (Baveno VIII 6.8). Her renal function and history of hepatic encephalopathy are assessed first, and TIPS is discussed together with liver transplant."],["What does TIPS do to right atrial pressure and cardiac output?","Both rise, because part of portal flow now returns directly to the right heart. That is why heart failure is excluded before TIPS."],["Why does ascites not improve after a Warren shunt?","Portal pressure stays high: only the gastroesophageal varices are decompressed, through the spleen to the left renal vein."]],sections:[["Spontaneous shunts",["Spontaneous"]],["Selecting patients for TIPS",["Selecting patients for TIPS"]],["The stent and portal flow",["The stent"]],["A surgical shunt",["A surgical shunt"]]],summary:"Spontaneous portosystemic shunts, TIPS indications and the pre-TIPS evaluation, the effects of the stent on portal pressure, hepatic perfusion and the heart, stent diameter, and the Warren shunt. Ends with a comparison of gradient, sinusoidal flow and hepatic encephalopathy risk.",slides:[{id:"own",lens:"flow",preset:"cirr-decomp",cam:"fit",sites:["split"],glow:["C1a","C1b","C3"],tool:{kind:"flows"},data:"tiles",tiles:["ppg","liver"],kicker:"Spontaneous",site:"sin",title:"Spontaneous portosystemic collaterals lower the gradient only slightly",vignette:"A 56-year-old man with alcohol-associated cirrhosis has ascites controlled on spironolactone and furosemide, and large esophageal varices. His platelets are 60,000/\xB5L.",eq:[r("<mn>1</mn>",s(a("R"),"total"))+i("=")+r("<mn>1</mn>",s(a("R"),"liver"))+i("+")+r("<mn>1</mn>",s(a("R"),"shunt")),"R resistance to portal flow \xB7 the liver and the shunt in parallel"],line:"Portosystemic collaterals divert part of portal flow from the liver, yet the gradient falls only slightly.",notes:"A shunt is a second path for portal blood, in parallel with the liver. Paths in parallel add their conductances, so any shunt lowers the total resistance, and with it the portal pressure, for the same inflow. In cirrhosis the collaterals open as the pressure rises, but the splanchnic inflow rises too, so the gradient stays high. The blood they carry bypasses the liver: hepatocyte portal perfusion falls, and gut-derived ammonia and other toxins reach the systemic circulation and the brain.",ask:["Why do collaterals fail to decompress the portal system?","Splanchnic inflow rises as they open, and their resistance is too high to carry it all at a low gradient."]},{id:"srs",lens:"flow",preset:"cirr-decomp",params:{spontaneous:{C6:!0}},days:30,cam:"splenic",mark:{edges:["C6"],label:"Splenorenal shunt",kind:"note"},tool:{kind:"doppler",vessel:"PV_TRUNK"},data:"tiles",tiles:["ppg","liver"],delta:"own",kicker:"Spontaneous",site:"sin",title:"A large splenorenal shunt diverts portal flow from the liver",vignette:"A 64-year-old woman with MASH cirrhosis has had recurrent overt hepatic encephalopathy despite lactulose and rifaximin. Her ascites has resolved, and EGD shows only small varices.",line:"A large spontaneous shunt lowers the gradient but diverts portal flow from the liver: main portal vein flow is now hepatofugal, at {pvVel}.",notes:"A splenorenal shunt joins the splenic vein to the left renal vein; with the retroperitoneal and paraumbilical shunts it is one of the large spontaneous portosystemic shunts seen on CT in up to half of patients with cirrhosis. A large one lowers the gradient and can reduce variceal bleeding, but it diverts portal flow from the liver: hepatic function worsens, portal flow may become hepatofugal, and ammonia reaches the brain. Large shunts are associated with recurrent or persistent hepatic encephalopathy, and embolization of the shunt can control it in selected patients. The tiles compare with the same patient without the shunt. Colored by flow, the portal and splenic veins carry blood toward the shunt, and main portal vein Doppler shows hepatofugal flow. The fundal varices that drain through the gastrorenal shunt, and BRTO, are in the Gastric varices talk.",ask:["A patient with cirrhosis has recurrent hepatic encephalopathy despite lactulose and rifaximin. What should you look for?","A large spontaneous portosystemic shunt, such as a splenorenal shunt, on CT."]},{id:"reasons",lens:"flow",preset:"cirr-decomp",params:{diuretics:!1},days:180,ramp:{albumin:[2.8,2.2]},cam:"fit",tool:{kind:"criteria",title:"Indications for TIPS",short:"Indications",ticks:!1,lead:"Each has its own criteria.",rows:["**Variceal bleeding** not controlled by drugs and **band ligation** (rescue)","**Rebleeding** despite a **beta-blocker** and **band ligation**",{text:"Recurrent or refractory ascites",note:"LVP every 10 days",met:!0,lit:!0},"**Preemptive**, within **72 hours** of a high-risk bleed"]},data:"tiles",tiles:["asc","ppg"],key:["asc"],vignette:"A 66-year-old woman with alcohol-associated cirrhosis, abstinent for 3 years, has needed LVP every 10 days for 3 months despite maximal diuretics. Bilirubin 1.6 mg/dL; no prior hepatic encephalopathy.",kicker:"Selecting patients for TIPS",site:"sin",title:"Four indications for TIPS",line:"TIPS is considered for variceal bleeding refractory to drugs and band ligation, rebleeding despite a beta-blocker and band ligation, preemptively after a high-risk bleed, and refractory ascites, as here.",notes:"Each indication has its own criteria, taught in the Choose the treatment and Preventing the next bleed talks. Here the indication is recurrent ascites. TIPS and liver transplant should be discussed together from the start, because TIPS for ascites is a bridge or an alternative, not a treatment of the underlying liver disease; refractory hepatic encephalopathy after TIPS may raise a patient's transplant priority. For a patient who is not a TIPS candidate, long-term albumin, a tunneled peritoneal catheter or an ascites pump may be considered. (Baveno VIII 4.13, 5.31, 5.34, 6.3, 6.4, 6.10, 6.11.)",ask:["Name the indications Baveno VIII gives for considering TIPS.","Variceal bleeding not controlled by drugs and band ligation (rescue), rebleeding despite a beta-blocker and band ligation, preemptive TIPS after a high-risk bleed, and recurrent or refractory ascites."]},{id:"path-asc",lens:"flow",cam:"fit",tool:{kind:"path",of:"ascites",short:"Ascites algorithm",patient:{tips:!0,lt:!0}},kicker:"Selecting patients for TIPS",site:"sin",title:"For her recurrent ascites, the algorithm starts with TIPS",line:"For recurrent or refractory ascites Baveno VIII recommends considering TIPS, with liver transplant discussed alongside. The stent is dilated only as far as needed.",notes:"The Baveno VIII algorithm for recurrent or refractory ascites (6.3 to 6.11), highlighted for this patient. She has needed large-volume paracentesis (LVP) every 10 days despite maximal diuretics, her bilirubin is 1.6 mg/dL and she has no history of hepatic encephalopathy, so she is a TIPS candidate. Patients with recurrent or refractory ascites should be considered for TIPS (6.3), and TIPS and liver transplant discussed together (6.4). Renal function is assessed and chronic kidney disease staged first (6.9). The stent may be dilated only to the smallest diameter that gives an adequate response, to limit overshunting (6.5), and dilated stepwise if the ascites does not improve (6.6); the next slides show the pre-TIPS evaluation and the stent diameter. For a patient who is not a TIPS candidate, the other branches of the algorithm are long-term albumin (6.10) and, if not a transplant candidate, an indwelling drainage device (6.11).",ask:["What does Baveno VIII advise for recurrent or refractory ascites, and what is discussed with it?","Consider TIPS, and discuss TIPS and liver transplant together. Dilate the stent only to the smallest diameter that gives an adequate response."]},{id:"check",lens:"flow",cam:"liver",data:"tiles",tiles:["asc","ppg"],key:["asc"],tool:{kind:"criteria",title:"Pre-TIPS evaluation",short:"Pre-TIPS",ticks:!1,rows:["No **heart failure**","**Renal function** staged (eGFR)","Prior **hepatic encephalopathy** reviewed, **rifaximin** considered","**Age** \u226570 or **frailty**: a relative, not absolute, contraindication"]},kicker:"Selecting patients for TIPS",site:"sin",title:"Assess cardiac function, renal function and encephalopathy risk first",line:"TIPS returns portal blood directly to the heart and, bypassing the liver, delivers gut-derived ammonia to the brain: assess both first. Age \u226570 or frailty alone does not exclude TIPS.",notes:"Baveno VIII lists no heart failure, age <75 and creatinine <3 mg/dL as the criteria for preemptive TIPS, asks for renal function to be staged before TIPS for ascites (6.9), and for the history of hepatic encephalopathy and medications to be reviewed (6.14). A history of overt hepatic encephalopathy is not an absolute contraindication (6.15); rifaximin should be considered before elective TIPS (6.16). Not absolute contraindications: for preemptive TIPS, acute-on-chronic liver failure, overt hepatic encephalopathy, an elevated bilirubin, the MELD score and severe alcohol-associated hepatitis (5.33); HCC unless the tumor lies along the planned TIPS tract (4.15); frailty or sarcopenia alone (4.19); and age \u226570 with refractory ascites, judged case by case (6.8). Each item is weighed with the others, and cardiac and renal function carry the most weight. TIPS and liver transplant are discussed together (6.4).",ask:["A woman with refractory ascites is referred for TIPS. Which three areas do you assess first?","Cardiac, renal and neurologic: heart failure, renal function, and the history of hepatic encephalopathy, with rifaximin considered."]},{id:"play",lens:"flow",cam:"liver",labels:["CONF"],procedure:{kind:"tips",then:"tipsgrad"},tool:{kind:"evgraph",title:"PPG and sinusoidal flow after TIPS"},data:"tiles",tiles:["ppg","liver"],key:["ppg"],kicker:"The stent",site:"sin",title:"TIPS is placed through transjugular access to the portal vein",line:"Through right internal jugular access, the catheter reaches the right hepatic vein. A needle traverses the parenchyma to the right portal vein, and a covered stent lines the tract.",notes:"A TIPS is an intrahepatic tract between a hepatic vein and a portal vein branch. Access is transjugular, so there is no abdominal incision. A covered stent keeps the tract patent: Baveno VIII specifies polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE)-covered stents. Once the stent is deployed, portal pressure falls immediately. (Baveno VIII 5.31.) Step 5 measures the portosystemic gradient across the new stent: the same catheter records portal pressure through the stent, is withdrawn to the IVC at the hepatic vein confluence, and records again. With a PPG <12 mmHg, a beta-blocker is not required after TIPS (AASLD 2024). The measurement immediately after the procedure can be affected by general anesthesia and vasoactive drugs (Baveno VIII), so it may not reflect the long-term gradient.",ask:["Which type of stent does Baveno VIII specify for preemptive TIPS?","A polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE)-covered stent."]},{id:"gradient",lens:"flow",params:{tips:{on:!0,d:8}},days:30,procedure:"tipsgrad",cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"reading"},kicker:"The stent",site:"sin",title:"The gradient across the stent is read against a target of <12 mmHg",line:"The catheter reads the portal pressure through the 8 mm stent, then the IVC pressure. The target after a TIPS is {<12 mmHg}.",notes:"Press Next to take the measurement. With the stent in, the catheter is advanced through it into the portal vein, where the portal pressure is read, then drawn back to the IVC for the second reading; the PPG is the portal vein pressure minus the IVC pressure. The target after a TIPS is a PPG below 12 mmHg (AASLD 2024: a nonselective beta-blocker is not required once the portosystemic gradient is below 12 mmHg). Baveno VIII Table 3 adds that the immediate reading can be changed by anesthesia, vasoactive drugs or hemodynamic instability, so a measurement in a stable, nonsedated patient better reflects the long-term value.",ask:["After a TIPS placed for variceal bleeding, what PPG is the target?","<12 mmHg, read as the portal vein pressure minus the IVC pressure across the stent."]},{id:"tips",lens:"flow",terms:["pv","hv"],params:{tips:{on:!0,d:8}},days:30,whatIf:n,cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"doppler",vessel:"TIPS"},data:"tiles",tiles:["ppg","liver"],delta:"reasons",kicker:"The stent",site:"sin",title:"TIPS lowers the PPG to <12 mmHg",line:"A covered 8 to 10 mm stent runs from a hepatic vein to the portal vein. The PPG falls to {ppg} (<12 mmHg).",notes:"The stent traverses the liver from the right hepatic vein to the right portal vein, giving portal blood a low-resistance pathway to the systemic circulation. The PPG is measured before and after; for variceal bleeding the target is <12 mmHg. On Doppler the stent shows continuous flow toward the heart along its whole length; a marked fall or rise in velocity from the prior study suggests stenosis. The tiles compare with the same patient before TIPS.",ask:["What does Doppler show in a patent TIPS?","Continuous flow toward the heart along the whole stent, at a velocity close to the prior study."]},{id:"heart",lens:"flow",cam:"liver",sites:["split"],tool:{kind:"profile",from:"reasons",fromLabel:"Before"},data:"tiles",tiles:["co","ra"],key:["co"],delta:"reasons",kicker:"The stent",site:"sin",title:"TIPS increases cardiac preload",line:"Cardiac output rises from {co@reasons} to {co} and right atrial pressure to {ra}. A normal heart tolerates this; a failing one may not, so heart failure is excluded first.",notes:"Portal blood that passed through the liver now returns directly to the right atrium, so venous return, right atrial pressure and cardiac output all rise. Baveno VIII names cardiac failure, with hepatic encephalopathy, as a risk of overshunting (6.5). The model shows the rise with normal cardiac function; it does not model a failing heart.",ask:["Why does TIPS raise cardiac output?","Part of portal flow now returns directly to the right atrium, increasing venous return (preload)."]},{id:"wide",lens:"flow",params:{tips:{d:12}},days:30,whatIf:n,cam:"liver",sites:["split"],mark:{edges:["TIPS"],label:"Stent at 12 mm",kind:"treat"},tool:{kind:"curve",param:"tips.d",from:6,to:12,step:1,lines:["ppg","liver"]},data:"tiles",tiles:["ppg","liver"],delta:"reasons",kicker:"The stent",site:"sin",title:"Dilate the stent only as far as the ascites requires",line:"At 12 mm the PPG is {ppg} and sinusoidal flow is {liver} of normal, with diminishing returns per millimeter. Baveno VIII advises stepwise dilation only if ascites persists.",notes:"The same patient with the stent dilated to 12 mm. The gradient falls further, but hepatic portal perfusion falls further too. Post-TIPS hepatic encephalopathy is more common with larger stents, older age and prior encephalopathy; liver failure is the feared complication when hepatic reserve is poor, as is heart failure. Baveno VIII says the stent may be dilated to the smallest diameter that gives an adequate response (6.5), and dilated stepwise when the ascites does not improve (6.6). Many centers place an 8 mm stent, or an underdilated stent that can be dilated later if the gradient stays high. Persistent hepatic encephalopathy can be treated by TIPS reduction. Underdilated standard stent grafts tend to expand partly toward their nominal diameter over about 6 months; controlled-expansion grafts are designed to hold the set diameter. On the figure, colored by flow, the stent carries far more blood than the sinusoids. (Baveno VIII 6.5, 6.6.)",ask:["Why are TIPS stents often left underdilated?","To lower the gradient enough while preserving hepatic portal perfusion and limiting the risk of hepatic encephalopathy and heart failure."]},{id:"occl",lens:"flow",params:{tips:{d:8},thrombus:{TIPS:1}},cam:"liver",mark:{edges:["TIPS"],label:"Occluded stent"},tool:{kind:"doppler",vessel:"TIPS"},data:"tiles",tiles:["ppg","liver"],delta:"tips",kicker:"The stent",site:"sin",title:"TIPS occlusion returns the gradient to baseline",line:"The gradient is back up to {ppg}, no lower than before TIPS, and variceal bleeding or ascites can recur.",notes:"Covered stents remain patent far longer than bare stents, but TIPS dysfunction still occurs, from early thrombosis or later stenosis at the hepatic vein end. It presents as recurrence of the original indication (rebleeding, ascites) and on Doppler as absent flow or a marked change in velocity. It is confirmed and treated at TIPS venography, with angioplasty or a new stent, and the gradient is measured again. The tiles compare with the patent stent.",ask:["How does TIPS dysfunction usually present?","With recurrence of the original indication, rebleeding or ascites, and an absent or changed Doppler signal."]},{id:"dsrs",lens:"origin",preset:"cirr-decomp",params:{dsrs:!0},days:120,lapse:{seconds:8,from:"Warren shunt",to:"Four months later"},cam:"splenic",mark:{edges:["S_DSR"],label:"Warren shunt",kind:"treat"},tool:{kind:"flowtime",clock:"days",lines:["ppg","varix"],keepTiles:!0,title:"PPG and varix size across 4 months"},data:"tiles",tiles:["ppg","varix","liver"],delta:"own",kicker:"A surgical shunt",site:"sin",title:"A distal splenorenal (Warren) shunt decompresses the varices selectively",line:"The splenic vein is anastomosed to the left renal vein and the coronary vein ligated. The varices decompress at once and regress over months; the PPG stays {ppg}.",notes:"The Warren shunt is a selective shunt. The splenic vein is divided close to its junction with the portal vein; the splenic end is anastomosed to the left renal vein, and the portal end is ligated. The coronary (left gastric) vein is also ligated, so portal pressure is no longer transmitted to the esophageal varices through it; the stomach drains through the short gastric veins and the spleen instead. Blood from the gastroesophageal varices drains through the short gastric veins and the spleen to the left renal vein, so the varices decompress at once, while superior mesenteric vein (SMV) flow continues to perfuse the liver at portal pressure. The operation also mobilizes the splenic vein from the pancreas and ligates the small pancreatic branches between them (splenopancreatic disconnection), together with the coronary and gastroepiploic veins, so pancreatic collaterals do not develop later and the shunt stays selective. The varices themselves regress more slowly, over weeks to months: they are decompressed at once, small by about 3 months and absent, or too small to grade, in most patients by 1 year (in one series the varices disappeared or decreased in 83% up to 10 years after surgery). Variceal rebleeding after a Warren shunt is uncommon, 5.5% in a randomized trial against TIPS, and when it occurs the shunt has usually stenosed or thrombosed, which repressurizes the varices. The PPG stays high, so ascites is not relieved and can worsen; refractory ascites is a contraindication. Hepatic encephalopathy is less common than after a total portosystemic shunt or TIPS. It is now rarely performed: it suits patients with preserved liver function and recurrent variceal bleeding, usually when TIPS is not an option. The tiles compare with the same patient without the shunt. On the figure, colored by origin, splenic blood leaves through the shunt, while the portal vein carries only mesenteric venous blood. The varices decompress through the spleen, and the PPG stays high.",ask:["Why is the Warren shunt called selective?","It decompresses the varices through the spleen to the left renal vein, while SMV flow continues to perfuse the liver at portal pressure."]},{id:"menu",preset:"cirr-decomp",cam:"fit",tool:{kind:"menu"},data:"tiles",tiles:["ppg","liver"],key:["ppg"],kicker:"A surgical shunt",site:"sin",title:"Every shunt trades a lower PPG for less sinusoidal flow",line:"From {ppg}, a drug lowers the PPG slightly and preserves sinusoidal flow. Each shunt lowers the PPG further and diverts more of that flow.",notes:"Each dot is this patient after one treatment, calculated by the model from the current state; the ring is his current position. The drugs move him a little, down and slightly to the left. A TIPS, a mesocaval shunt and a portocaval shunt lower the PPG well below the 12 mmHg line and sinusoidal flow to <55% of normal, with almost all portal flow bypassing the liver. A larger stent lies further down and to the left than a smaller one. Dot size reflects the share of portal flow that bypasses the liver. These are the model's numbers for one patient, not trial results.",ask:["Which treatment on the chart lowers the PPG the most, and what does it cost?","The portocaval shunt lowers the PPG to about 3 mmHg, and all portal flow bypasses the liver."]},{id:"summary",visual:"table",cols:["ppg","shunt","liver","he"],asc:!1,vs:"first",rowHead:"Shunt",of:[{id:"own",kicker:"Baseline",title:"Decompensated cirrhosis"},{id:"srs",title:"Splenorenal shunt"},{id:"tips",title:"TIPS, 8 mm"},{id:"wide",title:"TIPS, 12 mm"},{id:"occl",title:"TIPS, occluded"},{id:"dsrs",title:"Warren shunt"}],kicker:"Summary",title:"Gradient and sinusoidal flow with each shunt",line:"Every shunt lowers the gradient by diverting portal flow from the liver. The larger the shunt, the less portal flow reaches the liver.",notes:"Every shunt trades the same two things: the more portal flow it carries, the lower the gradient and the lower hepatic portal perfusion. Hepatic encephalopathy risk tracks the shunted fraction, and the last column is the model's index of that risk, not a clinical score. A spontaneous shunt is uncontrolled; a TIPS is placed electively, its gradient measured, and it can be reduced or dilated. An occluded stent restores the gradient and sinusoidal flow together. The TIPS rows are the woman with recurrent ascites; the baseline and Warren shunt rows are a patient with decompensated cirrhosis and varices.",ask:["What does every portosystemic shunt trade?","A lower portal pressure gradient against less hepatic portal perfusion and more hepatic encephalopathy."]}]};export{o as a};
